ite house in an  uncomfortable position both  diplomatically with israel  but also back here at home where we saw  a lot of  lawmakers democrats in congress you know  telling the administration that they  wanted the president to be much  more forceful to try to get the conflict  and  the deaths to stop and on that note  some democrats are proposing stopping in  arms sale by the u.s to  israel it was about to go through let's  talk about what the weapons proposal the  sale  is and why some democrats wanted to stop  right and the issue is that these  democrats were kind of working against  the clock here because they really had a  really limited amount of time something  like five days or so  to try to stop that arm sale it was  about 730 million dollars worth of jdam  so it's equipment that essentially makes  missiles more precise  and also from what we understand small  diameter bombs  and so a lot of democrats really  progressive democrats in the house were  saying we should not be  selling you know any type of arms to  israel at a time when there is such a  conflict happening  and that was alexander limon reporting  from washington d.c  the new chair of nevada's democratic  party did issue a statement about the  israeli conflict and it immediately  brought criticism  judith whitmer accused the united states  government of having quote  too long turned a blind eye to injustice  and violence committed by the israeli  government  dominated by the extreme right wing  unquote well that caused the newly  elected  treasurer of the democratic state party  to quit saying the state party needed to  be focused on electing democrats  and not sending out foreign policy  statements democrats from the  nevada's delegation responded all saying  they support israel's right to defend  itself so here's what congresswoman dina  titus told the items vanessa murphy  both sides have a right to live in peace  but  israel also has a right to defend itself  we're hoping for an end to the v